To avoid this, Claude kept each signal track under the wheel on a single layer, and it chose the chips’ pin assignments so that those tracks wouldn’t need to cross one another. It also kept the area around the Bluetooth antenna (the zigzag at the top) clear of other copper and holes on every layer, so nearby metal wouldn’t weaken its signal.

66
00:15:38.236 --> 00:15:43.823
Image description: Speakerphone circuit board, layer 4 (back): the touch wheel's electrodes.

67
00:15:44.273 --> 00:16:02.663
Figure 10: The voice-assistant hub’s four copper layers. Claude added two areas of grounded copper to the power layer (green), directly behind most of the tracks on the back of the board that carry high-speed signals. This gives the signals a nearby return path, which helps reduce interference.

