75
00:17:31.653 --> 00:17:49.486
Figure 14: The speakerphone’s circuit board can’t be lowered straight into the enclosure, because the mute switch’s knob would hit the back wall (red). Claude worked out how to install the board by tilting it about 25 degrees and then swinging it flat, and added this step to the assembly instructions.

76
00:17:49.606 --> 00:18:03.050
Image description: Cutaway drawing of the speakerphone enclosure showing the circuit board tilted at about 25 degrees as it is installed, with the point where the mute switch knob would meet the back wall marked in red.

77
00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.548
Limitations and next steps.

78
00:18:05.998 --> 00:18:27.735
All three of these devices are still prototypes: they haven’t been through electrical validation, antenna tuning, thermal testing, or certification, and they weren’t designed for mass production. Since Claude wasn’t connected to test equipment or to the machines that make and assemble circuit boards, engineers at Diode had to physically build and test the prototypes.

79
00:18:27.855 --> 00:18:49.679
Even so, over the past year, Claude has improved substantially at reasoning through decisions about component placement and routing, and it can now design electronics with minimal human guidance and help people build the prototypes. Other models are making progress here, too: OpenAI recently wrote about using its models to design a handheld device.

80
00:18:49.799 --> 00:18:54.352
Work like this could help engineers build and test new hardware designs faster.

81
00:18:54.802 --> 00:19:06.036
Next, we plan to investigate whether Claude can design denser, faster, and more complex boards, check them in simulation, and learn from measurements taken on the finished devices.

82
00:19:07.036 --> 00:19:24.774
That was Testing Claude on real-world electronics design, from Anthropic Research, published November 9, 2026. Read the original at anthropic dot com. This unofficial audio edition was generated automatically for the podcast Anthropic, Read Aloud.
